  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This study evaluates leave-one-out cross-validation (CV) for bandwidth selection in geographically weighted principal component analysis (GWPCA) using synthetic simulations across eight data-generating processes and two empirical datasets in Dublin and Kola. Results show that when covariance structure varies spatially, the set of bandwidths statistically indistinguishable from the CV minimum spans approximately 25% of the candidate grid, with the true oracle bandwidth frequently differing from the single estimated minimum. In practical applications, variance in local component loadings within this equivalent set concentrates in regions where the local eigengap is small.",
      "why": "It exposes structural ambiguity in standard GWPCA parameter tuning, demonstrating that relying on a single cross-validation minimum can yield arbitrary local loadings in regions with small eigengaps."
    }
  },
